WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:01.640
- [Carter] The average baby

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:05.350
will go through over 3,000
diapers in a single year.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.210
At around 70 to $80 a month,

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.250
the cost of these diapers

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.150
will set a family back
nearly $1,000 a year.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:16.030
And right now, this essential
is getting more expensive

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.090
for millions of Americans.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.200
In March, diaper manufacturer
Kimberly-Clark announced

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.140
that it would be raising prices on many

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.530
of its products, including diapers.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.530
And then competitor
Procter &amp; Gamble announced

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.110
it would be doing the same.

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.160
For families with multiple children,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.220
these price hikes could add up to hundreds

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.680
of dollars more per year.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.620
- These price increases are happening

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.280
on household goods, on food, on gas.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.120
Not only is it an amount that adds up,

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.450
it's an amount that comes in addition

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.900
to everything else costing more.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.750
- [Carter] So what's causing diaper prices

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.510
to rise so dramatically?

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:50.860
It has a lot to do with the prices

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.990
you're seeing here, and here.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.573
(lively music)

00:00:58.973 --> 00:01:00.980
♪ I'm a big kid now ♪

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.620
- [Narrator 1] Parents are
more likely to choose Luvs.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.730
- [Narrator 2] Pampers, the number one

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.290
pediatrician recommended brand.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.560
- [Narrator 3] We're Huggies,
we've got just what you need.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.110
- [Carter] It may look
like there are a lot

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.830
of options to buy diapers

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.330
but in reality, there are less

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:13.870
than what it may seem.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.120
Only two companies control about 80%

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.310
of the US disposable diaper industry:

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.840
Procter &amp; Gamble and Kimberly-Clark.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:21.820
The same two companies

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.370
that announced price
hikes earlier this year.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:25.390
- It means these companies

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.930
are less vulnerable to competition.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.120
- [Carter] Sharon Terlep
reports on consumer products

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.570
for The Wall Street Journal.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.750
- If P&amp;G makes a decision on pricing,

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.180
that might drive the whole market,

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.410
whether it's up or down.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.740
They don't have the concern as much

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.010
of having higher prices

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.760
and then having their
customers go somewhere else.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.280
- [Carter] Most disposable
diapers are made

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.930
with layers of blended materials

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.310
to increase absorbency,

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.590
including this fluffy part,

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.320
which is made from wood pulp.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.490
Just a few months ago,

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.600
lumber prices were through the roof,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:54.950
powered by low mortgage rates

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.810
and demand for home improvement projects

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.900
that drove a suburban building boom.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.400
This lifted the price
of wood, including pulp,

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.210
which in turn drove up costs
for diaper manufacturing.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.280
- Costs of logging have escalated due

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.650
to the inputs at that
stage in the value chain,

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:14.240
namely the labor and the logistics

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.820
and the equipment associated

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.080
with felling trees.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.650
- [Carter] David Garfield is a consultant

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.760
who has worked in the
consumer products industry

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.270
for almost three decades.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.750
- And those increases in costs

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.133
do ripple through the
production of pulp and paper.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:30.530
- [Carter] But one of
the biggest commodities

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.700
driving the price of
diapers is the same one

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.260
that is running your car.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.570
- Oil price is soaring,

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.410
an OPEC-fueled rally
sending crude prices higher,

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.160
now up about 20% just over the last month.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.140
- [Carter] As prices
have risen at the pump,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.130
so have costs to produce diapers.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.300
- Some of those ingredients or materials

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.540
that are incorporated into diapers

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.230
are derived from petroleum.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.810
And so with the increase in price

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.720
of the barrel of oil comes an increase

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.800
in the material that
goes into making diapers.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.150
There's also the knock-on effect

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:03.780
of diesel fuel prices up,

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.330
which impact the transportation costs,

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:07.840
as well as the material costs.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.410
- [Carter] Petroleum or crude oil

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.130
is the same raw material used

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.170
to make sodium polyacrylate.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.100
This is a polymer that's used in diapers

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:16.880
to help make them more absorbent.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.600
It's what these absorption
beads are made out of.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.600
Back in February, a winter
storm pummeled Texas,

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.050
forcing many petrochemical
manufacturing plants

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.770
to shut down, including those

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.230
that make acrylic acid,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.670
a compound in sodium polyacrylate.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.030
The extreme weather caused
acrylic acid shortages

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.950
for months, which drove up the price

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.400
of sodium polyacrylate.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.080
This contributed to the
rising costs of diapers.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.090
- Part of it was this piling-on effect.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:44.241
It was just one more thing
on top of everything else.

00:03:44.241 --> 00:03:47.350
But it made this material
harder to come by

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.760
and so at the time

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.110
when I spoke to Kimberly-Clark,

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.760
they said it's not driving a shortage

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.170
but it's certainly limiting options.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:03:59.930
- [Carter] But it wasn't
just limited access

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.950
to raw materials that have
driven up diaper prices.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.170
Throughout the pandemic,
ongoing shipping issues due

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.980
to a surge on online
orders, COVID restrictions,

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.620
and driver shortages have
caused significant delays

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.180
across the US.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.780
This has impacted deliveries
across industries,

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.670
including diapers, which has caused

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:18.290
freight costs to balloon.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:22.910
- You have the trucking
rates, the labor for drivers,

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:25.270
the equipment that needs to
be maintained and replaced,

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.210
even the insurance costs.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:31.810
So producers are seeing
significant increases recently

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.740
in overall transportation costs.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:34.980
- [Carter] Diapers, unlike some other

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.500
smaller personal care items,

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.430
are impacted more by shipping costs

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.370
due to the product's bulky size.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.850
- The vast majority of diaper production

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.220
in the US is local.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:48.740
There's a very, very small
percentage that is imported.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:52.320
It's a highly, what they
call, volumetric product,

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.720
meaning that per dollar of sales,

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:56.900
it's expensive to ship.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:01.550
And so it's largely
trucking transportation

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:04.250
within the US from the factories

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:05.390
to distribution centers

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:08.090
and ultimately through
retail channel partners.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:09.580
- [Carter] Still, there's one other factor

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.100
affecting the price of diapers

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.330
that has been trending for years.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.880
In the US, births have
been steadily dropping

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.490
for the past decade.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:21.090
The number of babies born in
2020 dropped by 4% compared

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.610
with the previous year,

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:23.930
partially driven by a trend

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.900
of women choosing to have
fewer children later in life.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.270
- As the birth rate declines,

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:31.020
it has an instant effect on diaper sales

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:34.330
because babies need diapers
the minute they're born.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.730
So if there's fewer babies born,

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:38.030
it's not some products

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.420
where 10 years down the road,

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.443
a smaller population might trickle down.

00:05:42.443 --> 00:05:44.651
If fewer babies are born in 2020,

00:05:44.651 --> 00:05:47.250
diaper sales go down in 2020.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.140
- [Carter] To make up
for lower diaper sales,

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.630
companies have increased prices

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.290
to partially fill the gap.

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:53.920
- To offset this decline in birth rate,

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.030
the companies are trying

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.840
to basically sell more expensive diapers

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.060
that are nicer diapers.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.860
So that competition has
become more intense.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.235
There's more organic diapers.

00:06:04.235 --> 00:06:07.110
There's a lot of features in diapers now

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:09.000
that didn't exist 10 years ago.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.720
- [Carter] As diaper
prices have increased,

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:11.940
for many working families,

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.960
the financial strain has added to a list

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:15.380
of growing expenses.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:19.270
- The need in 2021 seems

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:22.240
to be almost as high or
higher as it was in 2020.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:23.950
So even as the economy's recovering,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.420
it's not going back to 2019 levels.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:27.960
- [Carter] Experts like David Garfield say

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.030
that even if commodity and
shipping costs come back down,

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:32.650
because of increased margins,

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:35.350
diaper price increases
will likely stay inflated

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:36.635
for the near future.

00:06:36.635 --> 00:06:39.218
(lively music)

